  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy fog possible early this morning. No \nsignificant weather otherwise through Wednesday. \n\nAn expansive area of high pressure offshore is in control, resulting \nin warm south/southwesterly flow and tranquil weather. A stretch of \nnice mid-Fall days will continue through the middle of the week. \nWith some weak moisture advection, mostly clear skies, and light \nwinds tonight, some patchy fog may develop early this morning.  \nNot expecting widespread dense fog, however, but some areas \ncould see some lower visibility for the morning commute. Any fog\ndissipates by 9-10 AM and it should be a nice Wednesday with \nwarm temperatures in the 70s yet again. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A slow moving cold front approaches the area \nThursday night into Friday bringing the potential for heavy rain to \nportions of the area.\n\nUpper level ridging will finally begin to break down by Thursday as \na potent upper trough over the central CONUS cuts off and lifts \nnortheast into the Great Lakes region by Friday. This upper level \nlow is then forecast to slowly move east toward New England over the \nweekend. At the surface, low pressure over the Central Plains will \nlift north into the northern Great Lakes by Wednesday and up into \nQuebec/Ontario on Thursday. Its associated cold front will trail \nback to the southwest but will begin to slow down in forward \nprogression as it tracks toward the region before eventually \nstalling somewhere over/offshore of the Mid-Atlantic late Thursday. \nThe biggest question that remains at this time is where exactly and \nin what orientation does the front stall/wash out, as this will play \na major factor on how things pan out later Thursday and Friday. \nGiven the latest RRFS and ensemble guidance it is becoming \nincreasingly likely that the front will stall out within close \nvicinity to our area. Guidance is then keen on developing a surface \nlow along the boundary late Thursday night and into Friday while \ntracking northeast along the stalled boundary before lifting up into \nNew England on Saturday and pulling the front offshore. \n\nAll in all, this will yield the potential for some showers and light \nrain to begin on Thursday for western areas as the front approaches. \nBut as the front weakens, the forcing will too, meaning that many \nareas will stay mainly dry on Thursday as PoPs are generally less \nthan 30% along and southeast of the I-95 corridor. This changes by \nThursday night and into Friday, as a higher plume of PWATs will \nsurge northward upwards of 1.5-1.7 inches, which would push close to \nthe climatological max for early November. While not currently \noutlooked by WPC in the Excessive Rainfall Outlook, it is in the \nrealm of possibility that areas of heavy rain may occur from time to \ntime late Thursday night into Friday. The greatest potential of this \nthreat and where there may be a localized maxima of higher QPF is \ntargeted to be south and east of the I-95 corridor at this point in \ntime. However, it should be noted that this is highly uncertain and \ndependent on the placement of the stalled front. To summarize, a \nslower front will yield more rainfall across the region, in \ncomparison to a faster front which will yield less rainfall across \nthe region. \n\nRain chances should then decrease heading into the weekend as \nsurface high pressure attempts to build in from the west. However, \nas the upper low meanders across the Northeast, this is highly \ndependent on how lobes of shortwave energy evolve. Thus, while it \nhas trended drier over the weekend, there is quite a bit of \nuncertainty. The takeaway message here is that the weekend may not \nbe fully dry and could remain somewhat unsettled into early next \nweek with some diurnal opportunities for showers.",
